<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Hammadid emirate</h1><p class="st-meta">1008 CE – 1152 CE · capital Qal'at Beni Hammad</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Berber dynasty of central Algeria whose fortified capital, the Qal'at Beni Hammad, is now a World Heritage site.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammad-ibn-buluggin">Hammad ibn Buluggin</a>, a son of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zirid-dynasty">Zirid</a> founder, governed the western lands of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/zirid-emirate">Zirid emirate</a> and founded the fortified city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qalat-beni-hammad">Qal'at Beni Hammad</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hodna-mountains">Hodna</a> mountains around 1007 to 1008. In 1015 he broke with his nephew, the Zirid emir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badis-ibn-al-mansur">Badis</a>, and founded an independent dynasty.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Hammadid capital grew into a city of palaces, mosques and workshops, with a great minaret and fine glazed ceramics. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> ruled the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a>, drawing on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanhaja">Sanhaja Berber</a> tribes. Pressure from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-hilal">Banu Hilal</a> led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-nasir-ibn-alnas">al-Nasir ibn Alnas</a> to build a new capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bejaia">Béjaïa</a> (Bougie) on the coast in the 1060s, which became a busy port trading with Italian cities.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1152 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-mumin">Abd al-Mu'min</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bejaia">Béjaïa</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qalat-beni-hammad">Qal'a</a>, and the last emir, Yahya ibn <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-aziz">al-Aziz</a>, surrendered. Béjaïa remained a centre of learning and trade, and the ruins of the Qal'a were listed as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">World Heritage</a> site in 1980.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/hammadid-emirate.jpg" alt="Hammadid emirate" width="320" height="157"><figcaption>Image: Vonnlet · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Central_Maghreb_and_Ifriqiya_c._1065.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1008 CE – 1152 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Qal'at Beni Hammad</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Hammad ibn Buluggin, al-Nasir ibn Alnas, Yahya ibn al-Aziz</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Berber, Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/algeria">Algeria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hammadids, Hammadid dynasty</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1080&amp;focus=hammadid-emirate" aria-label="Hammadid emirate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4801 884 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Hammadid emirate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5121 1140L5255 1145M5121 1140L4794 1282M5121 1140L5255 1107"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5255 1145h0M4794 1282h0M5255 1107h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5121 1140h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5121 1140h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=1080&amp;focus=hammadid-emirate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
